{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the omitted lanes at head 6a5b87d4, tree d4f2f01e, for request ca74f78b and review request d0aa273b. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED, for one defect on the Work card.\n\n## The change I request\n\nWith more than six lanes, the Work card tells a person to do something that the card then refuses. `lanesLeft` in `apps/views/src/decision.ts` runs for every list screen, and `#rows` sets `next` for `work` too. But `work` takes no cursor, and `read` refuses a scope on it.\n\nI ran this on a scratch copy of the case's fixture, with nine lanes and this head's source:\n\n- `source.read({ screen: 'work' })` returns: \"This list leaves out 3 lanes. To read the lanes that follow, open this screen again with scope {…}.\"\n- Reading again with that scope returns `unavailable`: \"Only the needs-you and updates lists continue after a lane.\"\n\nFix the card so that `work` names the omission and offers no scope. It should say what the docs say, that `work` reads only the first window. Also drop `next` from `work`'s tool answer, or state in the docs that `work` can't use it. An agent reading `next` there has no `after` to pass. Add the assertion to the nine-lane case, with a control.\n\nThat changes source, so the next head needs a new gate.\n\n## Your four points\n\n1. A lane that opens behind the cursor doesn't break the claim for the lanes that the walk set out to reach. The order sorts on the canonical reference, whose first key is `inc`, so a new lane lands behind the cursor about as often as ahead of it. Nothing hides it: `omittedScopes` names that lane in every later answer, and the next walk reads it. A removed cursor lane still works, because the filter only compares. The note states this limit, but `docs/mcp.md` and the README only say that `next` is null at the last window. So add one sentence there: a null `next` ends this walk, and a lane that opened during it appears on the next walk.\n\n2. No, a stale card can't mark more than it showed. The session takes the capture from the card it displayed, and `checkedLocal` binds the action to that card. `markReviewed` reads each captured head again, and it refuses a moved head as `head-changed` and a lane no longer listed as `outside-room`. `begin` with `only` and no target selects nothing, and `read` admits each scope up to the eight-scope limit. In `review-store.ts`, `mark` updates or adds only the captured heads, so the first window's marks survive the second window's mark. The case doesn't assert that survival after the second mark, so add the assertion in the same head.\n\n3. It widens nothing for a view that doesn't set it. Both conditions in `session.ts` and `tools.ts` reduce to the old ones when the flag is absent. Outside session identity, only `declared.ts:100` reads `query.scope`. `decisionSource` uses the scope only as a position to compare, so it reads nothing at the named lane, and a reference outside the room selects lanes in this room only.\n\n4. Yes, the wording matches `judge.ts`. The exact retry answers first, at line 219, and that is \"answers with its first entry\". Expiry follows at line 224 on `clock.asOf`, and `revision-moved` comes later at line 302. Two rarer answers exist that the wording doesn't list, and \"can land\" stays true with them. `clock.behind` answers `unavailable`, so the record stays unknown. A lag beyond the lifetime plus the margin answers `bad-intent`. No change is needed.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The gate at 3f85721e, tree 2e0a88cb, exits 0: Plain 3,218 with one skip, Native 899 with two skips.\n- `git merge-tree --write-tree` of 074255e8 and this head gives this head's tree.\n- The evidence verifies. All 11 manifest rows match, the directory holds 44 files with none writable, and `SHA256SUMS` reruns clean with digest 5b3af9a0.\n- All seven control logs say that they distinguish. C4 and C6 are byte-identical, and so are C5 and C7, because each log prints only the first line of the assertion. So the logs don't show which assertion failed. I traced each of the four through the case by reading, and each fails where the note says. I did not rerun them. Next time, keep a line naming the failing assertion.\n- No gate ran on the tree with main 074255e8. That merge's source paths and this head's are disjoint, and only `docs/mcp.md` touches both sides. I ran the same owners as the note, and `s4.test.mjs`, `decision.test.mjs`, `security-entry-points.test.mjs` and `security-settings.test.mjs` pass 25 of 25. The new gate settles this.\n- `check-notes` gives 0 warnings, `check-doc-links` finds 775 links with 0 broken, `check-commit-messages` checks 8 with 0 flagged, and the worktree stayed clean.\n\n## For the planner, not blocking\n\nA cursor moves by lanes. In `needs_you`, a window over 50 rows or over the byte limit counts `omittedRows`, and no call reaches those rows. That limit was already there. A `rows-output-limit` error also reaches the card as `unavailable`, with no route to `next`.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: the stateless cursor fits, with the Work card fixed. It keeps `since` a pure read and serves `needs_you`, as your note argues.\n- Security: no authority widens. A mark stays local and bound to displayed heads.\n- Simplification: `listsAfterScope` is one flag with one reader in each file. Accepted.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:48cf3bbcc80d35f7976e3c450c794107bc21734c","binding":"assigned","head":"6a5b87d4d951b8a95ad49316543c68637ff3ed4c","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ac57190177a5fbef6885f33ac7ef91a478744cb5\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:04996c9877bd69fd490cf0d517f1d22c88fbd99a\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ac57190177a5fbef6885f33ac7ef91a478744cb5","review_path":"reviewguard@1","stale":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f75